    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Lorencez led 6,000 French troops against Puebla, certain that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would win the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> quickly, and chose to storm the most heavily armed and fortified point.</li><li>The French had long rifles against Mexican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> described as antiquated, but three uphill attacks on the forts of Guadalupe and Loreto failed and the French withdrew after a day of fighting.</li><li>The French won the Second Battle of Puebla on 17 May 1863 and went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, so the victory of 5 May 1862 delayed the invasion without ending it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Cinco de Mayo is not the national day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, as is often believed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The most important national patriotic holiday there is Independence Day, on 16 September.</p></aside>
    </section>

